  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏪 Fantasy Shop Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Magic shops, smithies, apothecaries and general stores, named the way real shops were."
    },
    "intro": "Sooner or later the party goes shopping, and a shop with a name feels like a real place. Fantasy shops borrow from real ones: named after the owner (Barthen's Provisions), the trade (Ollivanders, wand-makers) or a painted sign (The Bubbling Cauldron). This **fantasy shop name generator** builds names in all those patterns for seven kinds of shop, from magic shops to tailors.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Three ways shops get their names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The owner's name:</strong> the oldest pattern and still the most common. In Phandalin, the town in D&D's starter adventure Lost Mine of Phandelver, the party can stock up at Barthen's Provisions. In Skyrim, Belethor's General Goods is in Whiterun.</li><li><strong>A painted sign:</strong> like tavern names, older shops were known by their sign: The Golden Anvil, The Bubbling Cauldron. Arcadia's Cauldron, the Whiterun apothecary, mixes both patterns.</li><li><strong>A partnership:</strong> two names joined by \"and\". In Harry Potter, Flourish and Blotts sells books and Borgin and Burkes sells dark artefacts.</li></ul><p>The generator uses all three, plus simple descriptive names (Curious Curios) for shops whose owner is happy to let the goods speak.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Matching the name to the shop",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Magic shops</strong> can be a little mysterious: The Whispering Orb, Grimble's Oddities. A magic shop with a plain name is a hint that the owner has something to hide.</li><li><strong>Blacksmiths</strong> sound solid and honest: The Ringing Anvil, Gorrim's Ironworks.</li><li><strong>Potion sellers and herbalists</strong> sound green and old: The Mossy Mortar, Hettie's Remedies.</li><li><strong>General stores</strong> are friendly and practical: The Lucky Barrel, Barnaby's Provisions.</li><li><strong>Jewellers and tailors</strong> show off: The Flawless Pearl, The Dapper Thimble.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making the shop memorable",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Give the owner a quirk.</strong> The name tells players who to ask for: someone called Grimble is behind the counter at Grimble's <a href=\"wand-name-generator.html\">Wands</a>.</li><li><strong>Let the sign say something.</strong> \"The Dented Helm\" is either a joke or a warning about the quality of the armour.</li><li><strong>Add a motto.</strong> Ollivanders' sign reads \"Makers of Fine Wands since 382 BC\". A line under the name adds history in seconds.</li></ul><p>For the inn across the street, try the <a href=\"tavern-name-generator.html\">Tavern Name Generator</a>; for the town itself, the <a href=\"fantasy-town-name-generator.html\">Fantasy Town Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for a D&D magic shop?",
        "answer": "Something with a little mystery: an owner's name and their wares (Grimble's Oddities), or an adjective and an object (The Whispering Orb)."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I name a blacksmith in a fantasy town?",
        "answer": "Use the smith's name and trade (Gorrim's Ironworks) or a sign from the forge (The Ringing Anvil). Blacksmith names should sound solid and honest."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names for a real shop?",
        "answer": "Yes, but check local <a href=\"business-name-generator.html\">businesses</a> and trade marks first, especially for simple names like The Golden Anvil."
      }
    ]
  }
